WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.710
Film Courage: Have you seen people who have
supposedly “made it” (and we won’t mention

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:08.850
any names) but they feel like they haven’t
made it and that really with making it you

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.210
never really assume that you’ve made it
[or arrived].

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:18.280
That it’s one job to the next, dry spells,
failures, a success.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:23.220
That there’s not…”Oh yes, this is textbook
making it.”

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.939
Jack Perez, Filmmaker: That’s a great question.

00:00:25.939 --> 00:00:29.960
I don’t know about other people but I know
it in myself.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:36.329
I definitely know it in myself that I’m
still thinking about making it.

00:00:36.329 --> 00:00:39.870
I don’t know about other people because
I haven’t really spoken to…I have friends

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:47.250
of mine who are classically big success filmmakers
but I’ve never heard them talk about that

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.190
they don’t think they’ve made it.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:50.190
Maybe they have.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:56.109
I mean I think if you’re ambitious and you’re
shooting for something maybe you never really

00:00:56.109 --> 00:00:59.820
see what you have attained.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:05.000
And I think that for someone like myself I
know that there have been plenty of times

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:11.050
recently where I’ve been like jealous of
someone else’s success or feeling like I

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:16.240
got screwed and didn’t get to make enough
movies or get the kind of budgets I think

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.430
I deserved or things like that.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:25.600
And it really takes somebody on the outside
like my wife going you know what you are whining

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:32.130
about poor me but think about how many people
would like have given up their arms to have

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:33.420
done what you did.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.150
Sometimes it takes that kind of bucket of
cold water to go yeah yeah yeah you are right.

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:42.050
I am the luckiest guy on earth.

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:47.930
I think that the ambitious person they are
already looking for the next thing anyway

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:53.710
so if you are satisfied with what you’ve
done maybe you’re not going to do the next

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:56.070
thing anyway and you just go “Well I did
it.”

00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:00.670
But the thing that I think drives creative
people is this need to keep doing it.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:05.970
In a way you keep kind of canceling out, you
are forgetting what you did.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:07.520
But it’s not enough anyway.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:13.780
I think about this sometimes because we spend
all this time gearing up and thinking about

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:19.950
it and finally making the thing and then that’s
going to be over and then shortly after that

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.790
you’re kind of like “I’m not happy.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.700
I’ve got to do another thing.”

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.480
I remember seeing in that great documentary
IT CAME FROM KUCHAR about George and Mike

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:30.480
Kuchar.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.280
And the great experimental independent filmmaker
George Kuchar is making some crazy project

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:38.310
and they are filming him putting the last
touches on the editing.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:42.130
He makes the last cut and as soon as he makes
the last cut he goes “Now I’m depressed.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:44.510
I’ve got to make another movie.”

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:50.460
It’s almost that immediate, back to the
addict thing, you need that thing.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:55.569
So I think that attributes to a lack of perspective
which I know I’ve had where I’m just so

00:02:55.569 --> 00:03:02.540
concentrated on what I need to do next to
fulfill whatever it is that I’ve forgotten

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.530
that I’ve done some things that I should
be happy with.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:09.319
Like yes, you managed to accomplish that’s
good.

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:14.530
Maybe that’s more typical that not, I don’t
know?

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:20.209
Film Courage: Maybe in some ways that’s
a good quality to have - forgetting about

00:03:20.209 --> 00:03:26.800
your success a day after it happens because
then it’s going to keep you going to the

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:27.800
next, going to the next.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:28.800
Jack: I think so.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:29.800
Film Courage: Because if you are too happy
about it, that’s it.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:30.800
I don’t need it.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:31.800
Jack: Yes, I think it can be a nice balance.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:35.840
I think that in the time where you are not
getting…when things are falling apart.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.380
Like even if you’ve only made one movie
or short and you are struggling to make the

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:45.430
next thing and you feel like it’s never
going to happen you should still be able to

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:50.069
hopefully call upon what you have done, even
if it’s just written the screenplay you

00:03:50.069 --> 00:03:51.690
can say “You know what?

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:52.709
I accomplished that.

00:03:52.709 --> 00:03:53.709
It was not easy.”

00:03:53.709 --> 00:04:01.739
No everybody can do that, that’s something
to feel good about while I worry about that

00:04:01.739 --> 00:04:02.819
I worry about that I’m not making the next
thing.

00:04:02.819 --> 00:04:10.099
If you can take that information and use that
to support yourself and take care of yourself

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:15.069
I think that’s a healthy thing to remember
as opposed to just being in a constant state

00:04:15.069 --> 00:04:21.409
of want or a constant state of I don’t have
and I must…like a junkie again waiting to

00:04:21.409 --> 00:04:22.659
get well.

00:04:22.659 --> 00:04:28.409
It’s like in a way you almost need to remember
what you have accomplished and use that as

00:04:28.409 --> 00:04:36.370
a buoy or something to lift yourself up when
you do have those dry spells which are frequent.

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:41.449
Most of us never get to make a movie every
year or every two years or even every three

00:04:41.449 --> 00:04:42.689
years.

00:04:42.689 --> 00:04:47.610
It takes a lot of time and you have to have
something to sustain yourself other than misery

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.470
that you are just not making a movie.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:51.819
It’s a good question.

00:04:51.819 --> 00:04:53.150
It makes me think about it.

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:55.499
I’ve got to stop whining sometimes.

